1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Japanese saw — a sky full
of tools.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Japan took China’s Twenty-Eight
Mansions: the whole grid, but not the
names.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,000
In the Edo period, scholars gave each
mansion a Japanese reading. In the west:

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Tokakiboshi: a rice-levelling rod.
Tataraboshi: a bellows, a dictionary
says. Karasukiboshi: a plough.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:32,000
These are mid-Edo scholars’ readings,
not old folk star names. 11 of the 28
still have no agreed meaning.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,000
One is Subaru, the Pleiades. A
dictionary recorded it around 934.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The word means to gather scattered
things into one bundle.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
In 1955, a carmaker chose the name. Six
stars, six companies.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Karasukiboshi: Orion’s Belt, often with
the three small stars below, forms a
bent handle. A plough.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:09,000
The Bugis: a plough. Romania: a little
plough. Belarus: mowers. Siberia:
threshers. The Tukano: an adze handle.

11
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Unlikely to be borrowed. Wherever people
live by the sky, they see their own
tools there.

12
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Around 8 p.m., the Pleiades are high in
the south. Orion’s Belt, the plough,
follows to the southeast.

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The Blackfoot: lost children in the sky.

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

